                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Penrose-Hawking singularity theorems
            characterize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            in the theory of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein"} Einstein
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"}> general relativity
            ) which have “singularities”, points where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
            is undefined (or would be undefined if these points were included in the spacetime
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+spacetimes"} black hole spacetimes
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Hellman"} Hellman
            suggest this theorem as an example of non
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computable+physics"}> computable physics
            \. See
            %a{:href => "#Frank"} Frank
            for a response.
          %p
            A related problem is that of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Sbierski"} maximal Cauchy development for the Einstein equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            can be avoided.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+compactification"} conformal compactification
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+censorship+hypothesis"} cosmic censorship hypothesis
          %h2#references References
          %p The original texts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"}> Roger Penrose
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gravitational Collapse and Space-Time Singularities
                %strong 14
                (1965) 57 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.14.57"}> arXiv:10.1103/PhysRevLett.14.57
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+W.+Hawking"}> Stephen W. Hawking
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+F.+R.+Ellis"} George F. R. Ellis
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The large scale structure of space-time
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511524646"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511524646
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Large_Scale_Structure_of_Space%E2%80%93Time"} Wikipedia entry
          %p Exposition and discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Hawking"}> Stephen Hawking
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"} Roger Penrose
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Nature of Space and Time
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691168449/the-nature-of-space-and-time"} ISBN 0-691-03791-4
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9409195"} arXiv:hep-th/9409195
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Nature_of_Space_and_Time"} Wikipedia entry
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaas+Landsman"}> Klaas Landsman
                ,
                %em Singularities, black holes, and cosmic censorship: A tribute to Roger Penrose
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.02687"}> arXiv:2101.02687
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jos%C3%A9+M.+M.+Senovilla"}> José M. M. Senovilla
                ,
                %em A critical appraisal of the singularity theorems
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.07296"}> arXiv:2108.07296
                )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penrose%E2%80%93Hawking_singularity_theorems"} Penrose-Hawking singularity theorems
          %p For further developments see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Coley"}> Alan Coley
              ,
              %em Mathematical General Relativity
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.08628"}> arXiv:1807.08628
              )
          %p
            In view of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %ul
            %li#Hellman
              %p
                Geoffrey Hellman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical constructivism in spacetime
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/688083"} PDF
            %li#Frank
              %p
                Matthew Frank,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Axioms and aesthetics in constructive mathematics and differential geometry
            %li#Sbierski
              %p
                Jan Sbierski,
                %em On the Existence of a Maximal Cauchy Development for the Einstein Equations - a Dezornification
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1309.7591"} PDF
          %p
            Cautioning that the singularity theorem makes statements about non-extendible curves but
            %em not
            explicitly about “singularities” in the sense of points of diverging curvature:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roy+Kerr"}> Roy Kerr
              ,
              %em Do Black Holes have Singularities?
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.00841"}> arXiv:2312.00841
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Informed commentary is made in reply to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://physics.stackexchange.com/q/790724/5603"} Physics.SE:q/790724
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "https://physics.stackexchange.com/a/796154/5603"} Physics.SE:a/796154
          %blockquote
            %p Penrose’s theorem guarantees, under certain hypotheses, that spacetime is null geodesically incomplete. &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; The primary point of Kerr’s paper is that this theorem has nothing to do with the central ring singularity of his namesake spacetime. The theorem does not tell us that the ring singularity is there: the singularity could, in principle, be excised from the spacetime and replaced with a self-supporting stationary matter distribution without contradicting the theorem in any way.
        .revisedby
